A — Engineering guidance and recommendations
1) GaN vs SiC: practical recommendation
Short answer:
    • Prefer GaN for your likely voltage ranges (48–400 V) if you want highest switching efficiency, smallest gate charge, and highest switching frequency — and if budget allows.
    • Prefer SiC when you need ultra-robust performance at high bus voltages (≥600 V), extreme ambient temps, or when conservative, rugged devices and simpler gate drive behavior are prioritized.
Why:
    • GaN (eGaN, GaN-on-Si) advantages: much lower switching losses, very low R_ds(on) for a given die, low Q_g (gate charge) and fast switching — which reduces cooling and allows smaller passive components (smaller caps, smaller inductors). Great in 48–400 V domains for high-frequency drive and compact power electronics.
    • SiC advantages: excellent at high voltages and high temperatures, more mature for >600 V and industrial power; body diode and blocking behavior is robust. SiC parts are also well supported by cooling/packaging ecosystems for multi-kW systems.
    • Drawbacks: GaN can be more sensitive to layout and dV/dt; requires careful gate drive and EMI mitigation. SiC MOSFETs have larger switching energy at high frequency but are rugged.
Recommendation for your project
    • Use GaN for prototype/demo and final high-power density 1x1 designs where switching frequency and efficiency matter (48–400 V).
    • Keep SiC as a fallback when you move to higher bus voltage domains or when cost/performance tradeoffs favor SiC (large industrial motors / 600+V systems).
    • Always evaluate MOSFET package (thermal resistance, R_ds_on at operating temperature), and ensure gate drivers and layout are designed for chosen technology.
2) Capacitor sizing & placement — practical rules
Where to place capacitors
    • DC-link bulk capacitors: across the battery input, before the inverter / H-bridges. These handle the main pulse energy and steady energy buffering. Use low-ESR electrolytic or film caps sized for voltage and ripple current.
    • Local decoupling (near MOSFETs): add ceramics (X7R) + small film caps close to each half-bridge to handle high dI/dt and reduce loop inductance.
    • Supercaps / hybrid caps: for very large short pulses, consider supercaps or ultracaps (or a small high-energy buffer) in parallel with the DC bus.
    • After the H-bridge? The motor is inductive — generally you don’t put large capacitors on the phase outputs. Local snubber networks and TVS diodes go across switches or half-bridge nodes (not across the motor phase).
Sizing rule (simple, deterministic)
    • For a pulse of amplitude I (A) lasting dt (s) that you can tolerate bus droop ΔV (V), the ideal (minimum) capacitance is:
      C = I * dt / ΔV    (Farads)
      — Or compute needed C by energy: for an energy budget E = V * I * dt, then
      C = 2E / (V^2 - (V - ΔV)^2)
      (same physical basis).
Practical notes
    • If C is very large (fractions of a Farad), consider supercaps or battery-side strategies instead of only film caps.
    • Use a mix: bulk electrolytic/film + many ceramics (for HF decoupling).
    • Choose voltage rating >= 1.2× bus nominal. Check ripple current and ESR specs — these often govern actual performance more than nominal capacitance.
    • Keep caps physically close to MOSFETs to minimize loop inductance; use wide copper pours.
3) Snubbers, clamp and protection
    • RCD (RC + diode) snubber across switches or half-bridge node reduces energy dissipated in switching events (good for repetitive overvoltage).
    • TVS diodes sized to clamp occasional spikes.
    • Leverage synchronous switching with MOSFET body diodes, but still include snubbers or active clamps if there is energy recovery / regeneration.
    • RC damping + small series inductors can shape di/dt and reduce EMI.
    • For GaN: pay attention to dV/dt switching and gate drive layout; often you need dedicated gate drivers with proper turn-on/turn-off control.
4) Other controller components
    • Bulk DC-link cap (film or electrolytic) sized by the calc above.
    • Bypass ceramics: 0.1–1.0 μF per half-bridge node (X7R or better).
    • Common-mode chokes and EMI filters** at the battery side for emissions compliance.
    • Inrush limiter / precharge resistor + relay to avoid cap charging surge.
    • Current sensing: shunt + differential ADC or Hall sensors; place shunt low / in bus carefully for measurement and safety.
    • Bus precharge & contactors sized to battery current.
5) How much capacitance is “ideal”?
    • There’s no single ideal; it depends on:
        ◦ desired ΔV during worst pulses,
        ◦ pulse amplitude and duration,
        ◦ acceptable mass/volume/cost,
        ◦ whether you’ll use an energy buffer (supercap) or rely on battery internal impedance.
